{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120187",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093672/assets/9b6c8c113f59cc006f26e336b85abde6337a3af2a9f5d6dccc001eee636991ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{y}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{\u2032}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{\u2032}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
